  ('class7-math-en-chunk-11-006', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch11', 'en', 186, 187, 191, 192, 6, 'the corresponding over] 16. The heights (in cm) of 30 students of any class are given below. Draw the histogram of the heights and find the mode from the histogram. 145, 160, 150, 155, 148, 152, 160, 165, 170, 160, 175, 165, 180, 175, 160, 165, 145, 155, 175, 170, 165, 175, 145, 170, 165, 160, 180, 170, 165, 150. 2026 2026 Mathematics 187 Sample Questions Multiple Choice Questions: 1. What is the median of the numbers 10,12,13,15,16,19,25? (a) 12 (b) 13 (c) 15 (d) 16 For the numbers 6, 6, 9, 13, 10, 4, 8 — i. Mean is 8 ii. Median is 8 iii. Range is 10 Which of the following is correct? (a) i & ii (b) i & iii (c) ii & iii (d) i, ii & iii Answer to the question no. 3 & 4 based on the following information: The daily expenditures (in Taka) of 10 Students of class 7 are as follows 20,22,50,40,32,28,45,30,25,48 What is the median of the data? (a) 29 (b) 30 (c) 31 (d) 32 What is the mean of the data? (a) 29 (b) 30 (c) 31 (d) 32 Creative Question: 5. The marks obtained in matematics by 20 students of class VII are in the following: 50,60,52,62,42,32,35,36,85,80,81,82,47,46,48,43,49,50,56,80. (a) Ifa dataset has maximum value 71 and range 29 then, find the minimum value. (b) Make a distribution table taking 5 as class interval. (c) Draw a histogram from the obtained distribution table. Short Answer Questions: 6. (a) If the minimum value of a dataset is 17 and the range is 51, find the maximum value. (b) If the minimum value is 20 and the maximum value is 71, find how many classes will be needed with a class width of 8. (c) Find the median of the numbers 12, 10, 7, 9, 3, 11, 15, 10, and 9.', 309, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-001', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 20, 21, 25, 26, 1, 'Chapter Two Proportion, Profit and Loss We face many problems every day, which can be easily solved using the concept of ratio and proportion. So students need to acquire the concept of ratio and proportion and its application skills. Similarly, transactions and the associated profits and losses cover many areas of our daily lives. Because of this, students need to have a clear idea about profit and loss. Therefore, this chapter covers topics related to ratio and proportion, and profit and loss. At the end of the chapter, the students will be able to — Explain the ratio of multiple expressions and the successive ratios; Explain the concept of proportion; Solve problems related to proportions ; Explain what profit and loss is; Solve problems related to profit and loss; Solve problems of daily life related to tax, VAT, commission and money exchange; Solve problems related to time and work, pipe and cistern, time and distance and boat and tide using unitary method and ratio in real life. V VVVVV WV 2-1 Ratio of multiple expressions and successive ratios Ratio of multiple expressions: Let us suppose the length, breadth and height of a box are 8 cm, 5 cm. and 6 cm. respectively. The ratio of length, breadth and height = 8 : 5 : 6 In brief, length : breadth : height = 8:5: 6 Here, the ratio of three quantities is presented. The ratio of three or more quantities of this type is called the ratio of multiple expressions. Continued ratio : Suppose the ratio of ages of son and father =15: 41 (antecedent: subsequent) and the ratio of the ages of father and grandfather = 41:65. When two ratios are put together, we get son’s age: father’s age: grandfather’s age = 15:41:65. This type of ratio is called continued ratio. It is to be noted that the subsequent of first ratio which is the age of the father in this case is equal to antecedent of second ratio. If the subsequent of first ratio is not equal to the antecedent of second ratio, then they are made equal to find the continued ratio. 2026 2026 Mathematics 21 To convert two ratios into continued ratio, both the antecedent and the subsequent of second ratio are to be multiplied by the subsequent of first ratio and both antecedent and subsequent of first ratio are to be multiplied by antecedent of second ratio. Example 1. 7 : 5 and 8: 9 are two ratios. Express them as continued ratio. Solution : First ratio = 7: 5 Alternative solution : _7 first ratio =7:5=7x®:5 x® "5 =56:40 _ 7 x @_ 56 5x® 40 second ratio = 8:9 =8 <4): 9 x = 56: 40 = 40: 45 Second ratio =8:9 -8 9 8xO®_ 40 9x6) 45 =40:45 ..', 467, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-002', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 21, 23, 26, 28, 2, 'x @_ 56 5x® 40 second ratio = 8:9 =8 <4): 9 x = 56: 40 = 40: 45 Second ratio =8:9 -8 9 8xO®_ 40 9x6) 45 =40:45 .. Successive ratio of two ratios is 56 : 40 : 45 Activity : Express the following ratios as continued ratio : 12:17 and 5:12 23: 11 and 7: 13 19:25 and 9:17 5:8and 12:17 RYWNP 2-2 Proportion Suppose Shohag bought a packet of chips for 10 taka and 1 kilogram of salt for 25 taka a shop. Here the ratio of the prices of salt and chips = 25 : 10 or 5:2. Again, there are 70 students in the class of Shohag. Among them, there are 50 boys and 20 girls. Here the ratio of the number of boys and girls = 50 : 20 or 5 : 2. In both the cases, the ratios are equal. 22 Proportion, Profit and Loss Therefore, we can say, 25 : 10 = 50: 20. There are 4 quantities in this ratio. These 4 quantities have made one proportion. Among them if we consider first quantity as 25, second quantity as 10, third quantity as 50 and fourth quantity as 20, then we can write: first quantity: second quantity = third quantity : fourth quantity. Of the four quantities, if the ratio of first and second quantity and the ratio of third and fourth quantity are equal to each other, then the four quantities form a proportion. Each quantity of proportion is called proportional. The first and second quantities of a proportion will be of the same kind and the third and fourth quantities will be of the same kind. Hence it is not necessary that the four quantities should be of same kind. If two quantities of each ratio are of same kind, a proportion will be formed. The first and fourth quantities of proportion are called marginal quantities and second and third quantities are called mid-quantities. In proportion the symbol ‘: :’ is used instead of the symbol ‘=’. So, we can write 25: 10: : 50: 20. 4 marginal quantities mid quantities Again, first quantity : second quantity = third quantity : fourth quantity first quantity third quantity or second quantity ~ fourth quantity or first quantity x fourth quantity = second quantity < third quantity The rule of three We know, first quantity x fourth quantity = second quantity x third quantity Suppose first, second and third quantities are 9, 18 and 20 respectively. Then, 9x fourth quantity = 18 x 20 2 fourth quantity = 48x20 =40 x 1 “. The fourth quantity is 40 2026 2026 Mathematics 23 In this way, if the three quantities are known, fourth quantity can be determined. The method of finding the fourth quantity is called rule of three. Observe, e first and fourth quantities of proportion are called marginal quantities. e second and third quantities of proportion are called mid-quantities. Example 2. Determine the fourth proportional of 3, 6, 7.', 500, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-003', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 23, 25, 28, 30, 3, 'first and fourth quantities of proportion are called marginal quantities. e second and third quantities of proportion are called mid-quantities. Example 2. Determine the fourth proportional of 3, 6, 7. Solution : Here, the first quantity is 3, the second quantity is 6 and the third quantity is7 We know that, first quantity x fourth quantity = second quantity x third quantity 3 x fourth quantity = 6 x 7 2 or fourth quantity = #& XT or, 14 x 1 The required fourth proportional number is 14 Example 3. Determine the third quantity of 8, 7 and 14. Solution : Here, the first quantity is 8, the second quantity is 7 and the fourth quantity is 14. We know that, first quantity x fourth quantity = second quantity x third quantity or, 8 x 14 =7 x third quantity 2 third quantity = 8 x 44 _ 16 ra 1 The required third quantity is 16 Activity : Fill in the gaps of the following : (a) [J] :9::16:8 (b) 9: 18 ::25:L__] 24 Proportion, Profit and Loss Continued Proportion Suppose we have three quantities: Tk. 5, Tk. 10, and Tk. 20. We can form two ratios — 5:10 and 10:20 — with these quantities. Here, we can write 5:10: :10:20. This type of proportion is called continued proportion. Tk. 5, Tk. 10 and Tk. 20 are said to be in continued proportional. Out of three quantities if the ratio of first and second quantities and the ratio of second and third quantities are mutually equal, the proportion is called continued proportion. The three quantities are said to be continued proportion. If three quantities a, b, c are proportionals of the proportiona:b::b-:c, a_b ; ee . a: then —=— or, axc =)’. That is, multiplication of first and third quantities boc Second qua is equal to the square of second quantity. Observe : ¢ ntity is called mid-proportional or mid-quantity of first and third quantities. e The three quantities of continued proportion are of same kind. Example 4. If first and third quantities of a continued proportion are 4 and 16, determine mid-proportional and continued proportion. Solution : We know that, first quantity x third quantity = (second quantity)” Here, first quantity = 4 and third quantity = 16. 4 x 16 = (mid-quantity)” or, (mid-quantity)’ = 64 mid-quantity = /64 =8 The required continued proportion is 4: 8 :: 8: 16 and mid-proportional is 8. Example 5. If the price of 5 notebooks is Tk. 200. What is the price of 7 notebooks? Solution : Here, if the number of notebooks is increased, the price of notebooks will be increased. That is, the ratio of the number of notebooks = the ratio of the prices of notebooks 5:7=Tk. 200 : the price of 7 notebooks 5. Tk. 200 7 ~~ the price of 7 notebooks or, 2026 Mathematics 25 40 or, the price of 7 notebooks = 7¥K-200 = TK. 280. Pom 1 Example 6.', 497, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-004', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 25, 26, 30, 31, 4, '5. Tk. 200 7 ~~ the price of 7 notebooks or, 2026 Mathematics 25 40 or, the price of 7 notebooks = 7¥K-200 = TK. 280. Pom 1 Example 6. 12 persons can complete a task in 9 days. If the work is done at the same rate in how many days can 18 persons complete to complete the same task? Solution : It is to be noted that, if the number of persons is increased, then the period of time will be reduced. Again, if the period of time is to be reduced, the number of persons will have to be increased. Here, the simple ratio of the number of persons is equal to the inverse ratio of time 12 : 18 = The required time : 9 days 2 or YW _ Required time ; 18 9 days zB 3 or, The required time = 2 a days = 6 days — Proportional division Suppose Tk. 500 is to be distributed in the ratio of 3 : 2. Here, the sum of antecedent and subsequent of the ratio 3 :2=3+2=5 3 “. The first portion = 3 parts of Tk. 500 = Tk. 300 2 and the second portion = 5 parts of Tk. 500 = Tk. 200 propotional number of that part sum of the antecedent and subsequent quantities So, quantity of one part = given quantity x In the this way a quantity may be divided into various parts. To divide a given quantity into various parts of fixed ratio is called proportional division. Example 7. 20 metres of cloth is to be divided in the ratio 5: 3:2 among three siblings Amit, Sumit and Chaitee. What is the quantity of cloth for each ? Class-7, Mathematics, Forma-4 26 Proportion, Profit and Loss Solution : Quantity of cloth = 20 m. Given ratio=5:3:2 Sum of the numbers of ratio=5+3+2=10 Amit’s share = 5 parts of 20 m. = 10 m. 10 Sumit’s share =3 parts of 20 m. = 6 m. 10 and the part of Chaitee’s share = 2 parts of 20 m. = 4 m. 10 The shares of Amit, Sumit and Chaitee are 10m, 6m and 4m respectively. Activity : l. Ifa: b=4:5,b:c=7: 9, determine, a:b: c. 2. IfTk. 4800 is divided in the ratio 4:3: 1 among Aisha, Feroja and Khadija, how much will each of them get? 3. Tk. 570 are to be divided among three students in the ratio of their ages. If their ages are 10, 13 and 15 years respectively, how much will each of them get? Example 8. The ratio of income of Hasan and Masud is 4 : 3, and that of Masud and Sajid is 5:4. If the income of Hasan is Tk. 120, what is the income of Sajid? Solution: The ratio of income of Masud and Sajid is 4 j 3-4 = a = 29 99.15 3x5 5 The ratio of income of Masud and Sajid is 5:4 = 5 5x3 Gb.', 499, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
