 price of a car is 4 portion of cost price, what is the percentage of profit or loss ? 5 If an object is sold for Tk. 400, the loss is equal to the amount of profit that would be made if were sold for Tk. 480. What is the cost price of that object? . If the selling price of a watch is Tk. 625, the loss is 10%.', 482, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-011', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 36, 37, 41, 42, 11, 'made if were sold for Tk. 480. What is the cost price of that object? . If the selling price of a watch is Tk. 625, the loss is 10%. What would be the price of the watch if a profit of 10% is to be gained by selling it ? Maisha bought 15 metres of red ribbon at the rate of Tk. 20 per metre. The rate of VAT is Tk. 4 percent, and she gave Tk. 500 to the shopkeeper. How much taka will the shopkeeper return to her? Mr. Roy is a government officer. He will go to India to visit religious places. If 1 Bangladeshi Tk. is equal to 0.85 Indian Rupee, how much Bangladeshi taka would he need for Indian 42,500 Rupees ? . Nilim is a service holder. His monthly basic pay is Tk. 22,250. The income tax of two lac fifty thousand taka of first slab of annual income is Tk. 0. The rate of income tax on the next annual income slab is Tk. 10. How many taka does he has to pay as tax? 2-4 Speed related problems The speed of a boat in still water and in a streamy river will not be the same. In the case of a boat running downstream (with the current), the speed of the stream sohuld be added to the actual speed of the boat. In the case of a boat running against upstream (against the current), the speed of stream should be subtracted from the actual speed of the boat. The speed with which the boat 2026 2026 Mathematics 37 travels along with the current or against the current is determined as the effective speed of boat. So, effective speed of a boat along with the current (downstream) = Actual speed of the boat + Speed of the stream. Effective speed of a boat against the current (upstream) = Actual speed of the boat — Speed of the stream. Example 20. A boat can travel 6 km per hour in still water. Against the current, the boat needs thrice that time to travel 6 km. How long does the boat need to travel 50 km. along with the current ? Solution : The boat can travel 6 km in | hour in still water. . Actual speed of boad is 6 km per hour. Against the current, the boat can travel 6 km. in (1x3) hours or 3 hours According to the question, in 3 hours the boat travels 6 km. talon no » 2, or2km 3 .. Effective speed of the boat against current is 2 km per hour. We know, Effective speed of the boat against the current (opposite direction)= Actual speed of boat — speed of stream ..', 458, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-012', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 37, 39, 42, 44, 12, 'the boat against current is 2 km per hour. We know, Effective speed of the boat against the current (opposite direction)= Actual speed of boat — speed of stream .. Speed of stream = Actual speed of boat —Effective speed of boat. = (6 — 2) km or 4 km per hour Effective speed of the boat along with the current (same direction) = Actual speed of boat +Speed of stream. = (6+ 4)= 10 km per hour .. Along with the current, the boat travels 10 km in 1 hour 10 5 x50 yy 1 — oe » » «+ 30km_,, » of 5 hours The time for travelling 50 km is 5 hours. 38 Proportion, Profit and Loss Example 21. There are there pipes in a cistern. The empty cistern can be filled with water by the first and the second pipe in 30 minutes and 20 minutes respectivily. The filled cistern can be completely emptied by the third pipe in 60 minutes. (a) What part of the cistern can be vacated by the third pipe in 1 minute? (b) In how many minutes will the cistern be filled if all three pipes are opened? (c) When will the first pipe need to be closed so that the cistern is completely filled by the first and the second pipe in 18 minutes? Solution : (a) The third pipe in 60 minutes can vacate 1 cistern 1 2” ” ” 1 2” ” ” 60 2 Ans: a cistern * 60 (b) The first pipe in 30 minutes can fill 1 part 30 By the 2"4 pipe in 20 minutes, 1 part is filled 1 1 ” 2” ” ” 2999 20 2 And, by the 3° pipe in 60 minutes, 1 part is vacated 1 1 — ” vos ey 60 By opening all there pipes in 1 minute the part filled ( xt a - a ) part 2+3-1 =—~$0 part ~ 60 PAN 15 Pe i part is filled in 1 minute Therefore, 1. |, (+t)= (1x 15 _ 15 1 =15 minutes Ans : 15 minutes 2026 2026 Mathematics 39 (c) By the 2" pipe in 20 minutes, 1 part is filled 1 ] — » oo 0 0” 1x18 18 29 ed 29 ed Ed 20 29 = 7 part -_ 9 10-9 1 1 = Therefore, the remaining f 7 5) part 10 part 10 part 1 By Ist pipe in 1 minute, 39 Part is filled. To fill x part time needed = | minute 1x30 » tis os | I _ 1x30 » 10” ” ” 1x10 = 3 minutes Therefore, if the first pipe is closed after 3 minutes, the cistern will be filled with water by the first and second pipes in 18 minutes. Example 22. The speed of a 60 m. long train is 48 km per hour. How long will the train take to cross a nearby pillar of the rail line ?', 496, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-013', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 39, 41, 44, 46, 13, '22. The speed of a 60 m. long train is 48 km per hour. How long will the train take to cross a nearby pillar of the rail line ? Solution : To cross the pillar, the train has to pass the distance equal to its length. 48 km = 48 x 1000 m = 48000 m. The train crosses 48000 metre in | hour » 9 ” 1 yo _—! hour or 1*60x60 seconds 48000 48000 3 3 > 99 » 60, 1x66 x 60x 68 seconds , 43000 My, 9 = 2 seconds = 4i seconds 2 2 The train will cross the pillar in 4, seconds. 40 10. Proportion, Profit and Loss Exercise 2-3 Which is the twice divided ratio of 4:9? (a) 2:3 (b) 4:9 (c) 9:4 (d) 16:81 IfA:B=4:7andB: C= 10: 7, what is the value of C: B: A? (a) 49:70:40 (b) 49:40:70 (c) 40:70:49 (d) 40:49:70 What is the value of second quantity is successive ratio of 4:3 and 5:6? (a) 20 (b) 18 (c) 16 (d) 15 What is the continued ratio of 5: 3 and 2:5? (a) 10:6:15 (b) = 3:5:6 (c) 5:6:5 (d) 15:6:10 Which one is the fourth proporational of 3, 5, 15? (a) 20 (b) 25 (c) 30 (d) 35 A banana seller bought bananas at the rate of Tk. 25 per four bananas and sold at the rate of Tk. 27 He gained Tk. 50 as profit. How many bunches of four bananas did he buy ? (a) 25 bunch of four bananas (b) 20 bunch of four bananas (c) 50 bunch of four bananas (d) 27 bunch of four bananas 5 workers can reap crops of the land of 8 bighas in 6 days. How many days will 25 workers need to reap the land of 20 bighas? Swapon can do a work in 24 days. Ratan can do that work in 16 days. How many days will it take for Swapon and Ratan to complete that work together ? Habiba and Halima together can do a work in 20 days. They were working together, but after g days Habia went away. Halima finished the rest of the work in 21 days. How many days would Halima need to complete the entire work ? 30 workers can build a house in 20 days. 10 days after beginning the work, it was stopped for 6 days due to bad weather. How many extra workers will be needed to finish the work on time ? 2026 Mathematics 41 11. 12. 13. 15. 16. 17. 18. 19. 20. A and B together can do awork in 16 days, B and C together can do it in 12 days and A and C togther can do in 20 days. In how many days can A, B and C together do that of work? A cistern has two pipes. The first and second pipes can fill the empty cistern in 12 hours and 18 hours respectively.', 495, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-014', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 41, 42, 46, 47, 14, 'B and C together do that of work? A cistern has two pipes. The first and second pipes can fill the empty cistern in 12 hours and 18 hours respectively. If both pipes are opened together, how much time will the empty cistern need in order to be filled? A boat can cross 36 km in 4 hours along with current. If the speed of current is 3 km/hour, what is the speed of boat in still water? A ship can travel 77 km of waterway in 11 hours against the current. If the speed of the ship is 9 km/hour in still water, what is the speed of the current per hour? A boat can travel with the help of an oar 3 km in 15 minutes along with current and against the current it travels 1 km in 15 minutes. Determine the speed of the boat in still water and the speed of the current. Lily can do a work in 10 hours alone. Mily can do that work in 8 hours. How many hours will Lily and Mily take to complete the work together? Two pipes separately can fill an empty cistern with water in 20 minutes and 30 minutes respectively. The cistern is completely empty and both the pipes are opened together to fill the cistern. When will the first pipe need to be closed so that the cistern would be completely filled in 18 minutes? The speed of a 100 metre long train is 48 km/hour. That train can cross a bridge in 30 seconds. What is the length of the bridge? A 120 metre long train will cross 330 metre long bridge. If the speed of the train is 30 km per hour, how much time will the train need to cross the bridge? A piece of ornament is made by mixing bronze, zinc and silver. In that piece of ornament, the ratio of bronze and zinc is 1:2 and the ratio of zinc and silver is 3:5. The weight of the ornament is 190 grams. (a ) Determine the ratio of bronze, zinc and silver. (b) Determine the weights of bronze, zinc and silver in the ornament separately. (c) What weight of zinc can be mixed in the ornament so that the ratio of bronze and zinc will be 1:3? Class-7, Mathematics, Forma-6 42 Proportion, Profit and Loss Sample Questions Multiple Choice Questions: 1. A shopkeeper bought a match box at Tk. 1.50 and sold at Tk. 2.00. What is the percentage of profit? (a) 20% (b) 15% (c) 25% (d) 335% If two ratios are 2:3 and 8:12, then — (i) The antecedent (first term) of the second ratio is 8 (ii) The continued proportion of the two ratios is 16:24:36 (iii) The four terms of the two ratios form a proportion Which of the following is correct? (a) i & ii (b) i & iii (c) ii & iti (d) i, ii & iti Answer the question no.', 497, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-2-015', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch2', 'en', 42, 42, 47, 47, 15, 'form a proportion Which of the following is correct? (a) i & ii (b) i & iii (c) ii & iti (d) i, ii & iti Answer the question no. 3 & 4 based on following informatton: 30 metre cloth are distributed amon Maisa, Maria and Tania in the ratio 5 : 3: 2. 3. How many metre of cloth did Maisa get? (a) 15 (b) 9 (c) 6 (d) 5 How Many metre of cloth did Maria get more than Tania? (a) 3 (b) 5 (c) 6 (d) 9 Creative Question: 5. A businessman sells a calculator for 1600 taka with a 25% profit and sells a watch for 800 Taka at a loss. If the watch had been sold for 1200 taka, he would have made a profit three times the amount of the loss. (a) Write the numbers 5 cm, 7 cm, 14 cm, and 10 cm in proportion. (b) Determine how much profit the businessman made from selling the calculator. (c) Determine the percentage loss the businessman incurred by selling the watch for 800 taka. Short Answer Questions: 6. (a) A total of 60 Taka was distributed between Omi and Sumi. If Omi received 12 Taka more than Sumi, find the ratio of the amounts they received. (b) If the ages of three out of four people in continuous proportion are 11 years, 16 years, and 22 years, find the age of the fourth person. (c) If an item is bought for 120 taka and sold for a profit of 20 taka, find the ratio of the cost price to the selling price. (d) A tank has two pipes: the first pipe fills 1/4 of the tank in 5 minutes, and the second pipe fills half of the tank in 15 minutes. Determine which pipe can fill the tank in less time. 2026', 307, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-001', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 43, 44, 48, 49, 1, '2026 Chapter Three Measurement In real life, we constantly use different types of objects. Measurement is the determination of the quantity of those objects. Generally we measure length, weight, area and volume etc, in the case of solid objects. However, since liquids do not have a specific shape, the amount of liquid is determined by keeping it in a container and determining the volume of the container. In this chapter we will discuss in detail the measurement of length, area, weight and volume of liquids. At the end of this chapter, the students will be able to — > Explain the interrelation of measuring length and solve the related problems; > Explain how weight and volume of liquid are measured and solve the related problems; > Determine the area of rectangle and square by measuring of length and width using a scale; > Measure the weight of goods by using different measuring units of weight; Measure volume of liquids using different measuring units of volume; Estimate measurements of things in our everyday life approximately. Vv 3-1 Measurement of Length We buy clothes, electric wire, rope etc., from market. These are bought and sold by comparing with a standard length. Again,we need to know the distance of a school, bazar or station from our home. This distance is also found by comparing with the same standard length. This standard length is known as the unit of measurement of length. There are two systems of measuring length. 1. British system 2. Metric system. In British system yard, foot and inch are in use as the units of measurement of length. However, at present most of the countries of the world use metric system. In metric system metre, centimetre, kilometre are in use as the units of measurement of length. One ten-millionth part of the length from the North Pole to the Equator along the latitude passing through Paris, is considered to be 1 metre. 44 Measurement Inthe metric system metre is the unit of measuring length. 1 metre = One ten-millionth part of the length from the North pole to the Equator. .| North Pole Throughout the world the metal scale made of admixture of Platinum and Uranium is considered as the ideal or standard scale. It is kept in French Science museum. For the necessity of different countries local scale is made from the standard scale to measure exactly. Observe that, for measuring lengths weights and volume of liquids, Bangladesh has introduced International Standard or System of International Unit (SI) in1982.', 420, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-002', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 44, 46, 49, 51, 2, 'made from the standard scale to measure exactly. Observe that, for measuring lengths weights and volume of liquids, Bangladesh has introduced International Standard or System of International Unit (SI) in1982. Units of measurement of length Metric system British system 10 millimetres(mm) = 1centimetre(cm) | 12 inches = 1 foot 10 centimetres = ldecimetre (dm) | 3 feet = lL yard 10 decimetres = 1 metre (m) 1760 yards = 1 mile 10 metres = 1 decametre (dam) 10 decametres = 1 hectometre (hm) 10 hectometres = | kilometre (km) Relation between Metric and British system 1 inch = 2-54 centimetres (approx.) 1 mile = 1-61 kilometres (approx.) 1 metre = 39-37 inches (approx.) 1 kilometre = 0-62 miles (approx.) 2026 2026 Mathematics 45 Activity: 1. Give examples of a few things of daily use that we measure by length. 2. Measure the length and width of a book and a table in inches and centimetres with a ruler. Find from it how much centimetres are equal to 1 inch. 3. Measure the length and width of your classroom with a measuring tape. 3-2 Measurement of Area The concept of measurement of area is very important in our life. Our dwelling houses, educational institutions, hospitals and various institutions etc. are important constructions for us. We need to know the area of the land on which these institutions are built . An enclosed space is a region and the measurement of the region is area. Every region usually has length and width. So for the measurement of area, a square with a side of 1 unit length is taken as the unit of area. The unit of area is square unit. The area of a square of side 1 metre is square 1 metre. Similarly, 1 square foot, 1 square centimetre etc are also used as units of area. 1 unit 1 square unit 1 unit In measuring the area of a region, one needs to find out how many units fit in the region. Let the length of a side of a square be 1 metre. Therefore, the area of the square is 1 sq. metre. Each of the sides of the square is divided into ten equal parts and the opposite points of division are joined together. < 1Metre 4 «1 decimetre = lcm € > t + lcm 1 Metre 1 decimetre —____ >» | ——> n 1 + ; Fig :3 Fig: 1 Fig :2 46 Measurement In figure 1 the length of sides of each small square is 1 decimetre. From the figure 2, it is visible that there are 100 very small squares in 1 small square in figure 1. So, 1 square metre = 100 square decimetres. Similarly, considering the square with side of 1 decimetre in length and dividing into 10 equal parts as before, it can be shown that the area = 1 square decimeter =10 centimetres x 10 centimetres or 100 square centimetres.', 491, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-003', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 46, 48, 51, 53, 3, 'in length and dividing into 10 equal parts as before, it can be shown that the area = 1 square decimeter =10 centimetres x 10 centimetres or 100 square centimetres. So, 1 square metre =100 x 100 square centimetres = 1 10000 square centimetres. Observe, the meaning of 4 metres square is not the same as 4 square metres. 4 metre square means a square region with a side of 4 metres in length and whose area is (4x4) or 16 square metres. But 4 square metres means the area of region is 4 square metres. Formulae for area of some regions: Rectangle Area of the rectangle region b (breadth) = length x breadth =I1xb / (length) Parallelogram h(height) Area of the parallelogram region = base x height =I1xh / (base) Triangle (height) Area of the triangle region 1 = 5 x base x height b(base) 1 —x(bxh 5 (b xh) 2026 2026 Mathematics 47 Relation between Metric and British system in measuring area British system Local system 1 sq. inch = 6.45 sq.centimetres (approx.) | | 1sq.centimetre = 0.155 sq.inches (approx.) 1 sq. foot = 929 sq. centimetres (approx.) ||1sq.metre = 10.76 sq. feet (approx.) 1 sq. yard = 0.84 sq. metres (approx.) 1 hectre = 2.47 acres (approx.) Activity 1. Measure the length and breadth of a book and a table in centimeters with a ruler and find their areas. 2. In groups measure the length and breadth of benches, tables, doors, windows etc with aruler and find their areas. 3.3 Measurement of weights Every object has its own weight. To measure the weight different units are used in different countries. In metric system gram is an unit of measurement of weight. The weight of 1 cc. of purified water at 4° Celsius is equal to 1 gram There are two more units for measurement of weights. These units are used for measurement of large quantities of goods. The units are quintal and metric ton. Units of measurement of weight 10 milligrams (mm) = 1centigram(cm) 10 centigrams = 1 decigram (dg) 10 decigrams = 1 gram (gm) 10 grams = 1 decagram (dag) 10 decagrams = | hectogram (h8) 10 hectograms or (kgs) = I kilogram (kg) 1 kilogram (kg) = 1000 grams 100 kilograms = | quintal 1000 kilograms or 10 quintals =| metric ton 48 Measurement In cities and villages, scales and weights are used for measuring weight of goods. The weights are of 5 gm, 10 gm, 50 gm, 100 gm, 200 gm, 500 gm, 1 kg, 2kg, 5 kg, 10 kg etc. Now a days in cities weights are measured using graduated balances. These balances look like the lower part of a truncketed pyramid. Goods to be weighed are kept on top of the balance and one of the lateral sides is graduated like a clock. The pointer of the balance moves clockwise like the minutes’ hand of a clock.', 486, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-004', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 48, 50, 53, 55, 4, 'on top of the balance and one of the lateral sides is graduated like a clock. The pointer of the balance moves clockwise like the minutes’ hand of a clock. When something is placed on the balance, the digit at the tip of the pointer represents its weight. Here each kg is divided into 10 parts and identified this part with lines. blo Tv] Digital balances Graduated balances Presently, digital balances are replacing the graduated balances. The digital balance looks like a small box with a digital display on one side. The weight is measured in grams. Some of them are capable of calculating the price of goods to be measured just like a calculator. For this reason, the use of these balances is more convenient. However, for measurement of large amount of goods, classical balances are still in use. Activity : Use a normal or digital balance to measure the weight of your ruler, books, tiffin box etc. in groups and write them in metric units. 3-4 Measuring Volume of Liquids The space occupied by liquid is the volume of the liquid. A solid has length, width and height. But liquid has no definite shape. It takes the shape of its container. So liquids are measured by a measuring pot of definite volume. Usually we use a1 litre measuring container. These measuring containers Mathematics 49 are conical or cylindrical shaped aluminium mugs of 7 3° 1, 2, 3, 4.0... etc litres. Again, vertical container made of transparent glass marked by 25, 50, 100, 200, 300, 500, 1000 millilitres are widely used. Usually these pots are used while measuring milk and oil. | Litre Mug 1 Liter Graduated Mug Now-a-days for the convenience of consumers edible oil is sold in bottles. The bottles of 1, 2, 5 and 8 liter are used widely . A variety of soft drinks are sold in 250, 500, 1000, 2000 millilitre or other volume of bottles. 4 a 4 nny ry / ih 4 q =a an 5 Litres Bottle | Litre Bottle In English 1 cubic centimetre is abbreviated as cc. 1 cubic centimetre (cc)= 1 millilitre| 1 cubic inch = 16.39 millilitres (approx.) Metric units of measurement of volume 1000 cubic centimetres (cc) = 1 cubic decimetre 1000 cubic decimetres = 1 cubic metre 1000 cubic centimetres = 1 litre 1 litre of water (weight) = 1 kilogram Activity 1. Measure the capacity of a water container in c.c. 2. Assume the volume of a container of unknown volume. Then measure the volume of the container and determine the extent of error in your assumption. Class-7, Mathematics, Forma-7 50 Measurement Example 1. If 420 metric tons of potatoes are produced in 16 acres of land, then how much potatoes are produced in | acre of land? Solution: 16 acres of land produce 420 metric tons of potatoes , 420 16 sy " " " " = 26+ metric tons or 26 metric tons 250 kg. ..', 494, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-005', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 50, 51, 55, 56, 5, 'Solution: 16 acres of land produce 420 metric tons of potatoes , 420 16 sy " " " " = 26+ metric tons or 26 metric tons 250 kg. .. The production of potato per acre is 26 metric tons 250 kg. Example 2. Raihan produces 400 kg paddy from one acre of land. If he gets 700 gm of rice out of 1 kg of paddy, What quantity of rice does he get ? Solution: 1 kg of paddy gives 700 gm of rice 400" """" 700x400" " = 280000 gm = 280 kg .. The obtianed amount of rice is 280 kg. Example 3. A car burns 10 litres of diesel to run 80 km. How much diesel does it require to run 1 kilomtre? Solution: The car runs 80 kilimetres by burning 10 litre of diesel " 10 " noms 1000 millilitres or 125 80 8 " " 1 " millilitres of diesel .. The required volume of diesel is 120 millilitres. Example 4. The base and height of a triangular field are 6 metres and 4 metres respectively. What is the area of the triangular region? 1 Solution: The area of the triangular region = 3 x (base x height) = ; x (6 x4) sq. metres = 12 sq. metres 2026 .. The required area of the triangular region is 12 square metres. 2026 Mathematics 51 Example 5. The area of a triangular field is 216 sq. meter and its base is 18 meters. What is the height of the triangle? Solution: We know that 1 . . 3 x base x height = Area of the triangle 1 or, 3 x 18 metres x height = 216 sq. metres or, 9 metres x height = 216 sq. metres or, height = 3 metres or 24 .. The required height is 24 metres. Example 6. A pond with banks is 80 metreslong and 50 metres wide. If the width of the bank on all sides is 4 metres, what is the area of the banks? Solution: 80m The length of the pond excluding banks = {80 — (4 x 2)} metres = 72 metres The width of the pond excluding banks = {50 — (4 x 2)} metres = 42 metres The area of the pond including banks = (80 x 50) sq. metres = 4000 sq. metres The area of the pond excluding banks = (72 = 42) sq. metres = 3024 sq. metres .. The area of the banks = (4000 — 3024) sq. metres = 976 sq. metres 50m 4m -. The area of the banks is 976 sq. metres. Example 7. The perimetre of a rectangular house is equal to the perimeter of a square house. The length of the rectangular house is 3 times its breadth. The cost to cover the floor with a carpet is Tk. 11025 at the rate of Tk. 75 per sqmetre.', 486, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-006', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 51, 53, 56, 58, 6, 'length of the rectangular house is 3 times its breadth. The cost to cover the floor with a carpet is Tk. 11025 at the rate of Tk. 75 per sqmetre. (a) It the breadth is ''A'', find the area of the rectangular house is terms of ''A''. (b) Determine the length and breadth of the rectangular house. (c) How many tiles each having an area of 40 sq. cm will be needed to cover the floor of the square house? 52 Measurement Solution : (A) Let the breadth of the reetangular house be ''A'' metre. Length = 3A metres Therefore, Area = Length x Breadth= (A x A) sq. metres =3A2 sq. metres (b) Tk 75 is spent to cover 1sq.m of floor of the house (c) 1 “99 ” ” an ” ” 715 1x11025 715 ” ” = 147 sq.m floor of the house "99 11025 ” ” Therefore, the area of the floor is 147 sq. metres As per question, 3A2= 147 [got from''A''] or, A2= “a or, A2= 49 or, A =/49 =7 metres Therefore, the breadth of the house is 7 metres Therefore, the length of the house = 3A m = (3 x 7) = 21 metres Ans. Length 21 metres, breadth 7 metres. From part (b), the length of the rectangalar house is 21 metres and breadth is 7 metres. The perimeter of the rectangular house =2 (21+7) metre = 56 metres The perimeter of the square house = 56 metres The length of side of square house * metres = 14 metres The area of floor of square =(14 x 14)sq.metres=196 sq.metres Area of a square tile = 40 cm x 40 cm =0.4m x 0.4 m= 0.16 sq. metres Therefore, the number of tiles needed to cover the floor is 016 = 1225 2026 2026 Mathematics 53 Exercise 3 1. If the length of one edge of a cube is 3 metres, which one of the following is total surface area of the cube? (a) 54 sq. metres (b) 18 sq. metres (c) 9 sq. metres (d) 9 sq. metres Answer Question no. 2 & 3 based on the following information: The length of a rectangular garden is three times of the breadth. One full walk around the garden is equal to 400 metres. 2. What is the length of the garden in metres? (a) 50 (b) 100 (c) 150 (d) 200 3. | How much sq. metres is the area of the garden? (a) 400 (b) 2500 (c) 5000 (d) 7500 4. What is the meaning of deci in Latin? (a) One fifth (b) One tenth (c) One thousandth (d) One hundreth 5. Express in kilometres: (a) 40390 cm (b) 75metres 250 mm Express 5.37 decametres in metres and decimetres. Determine the area of each triangle with the following bases and heights: (a) base 10m and height 6m (b) base 25 cm and height 14 cm 8. The length of a rectangular plot is 3 times of its breadth.', 499, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-007', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 53, 54, 58, 59, 7, 'bases and heights: (a) base 10m and height 6m (b) base 25 cm and height 14 cm 8. The length of a rectangular plot is 3 times of its breadth. A walk around the plot measures | km. Determine the length and breadth of the plot. 9. What is the cost of fencing around a 100-metre long and 50-metre wide rectangular park at arate of Tk. 100 per metre ? 10. The base and height of a parallelogram are 40 metres and 50 metres respectively. Determine the area of the parallelogram. 11. The length of one edge of a cube is 4 metres . Determine the total surface area of the cube. 12. Abul produces 500 kg 700 gm of potatoes in a piece of land. How much potatoes will be produced in 11 pieces of land of an equal area ? 54 13. 20. 21. 22. 22. Measurement 28 metric ton of paddy were produced in Mofiz’s 16 acres of land. What was the production of paddy per acre ? . Ina steel mill 200000 metric tons of rod are produced in a month. What is the average daily production of the mill? . A merchant sells 20 kg 400 gm of lentil per day. On an average how much lentil does he sell each month ? . 20kg 850 gm of of mustard is produced in a piece of land. What amount is produced if the land is divided into 7 equal parts? . The volume of a mug is 1500 cu. cm. How many mugs of water are needed to hold 270 litres of water? . A merchant sells 18 kg 300 gm of rice and 5 kg 750 gm of salt each day on an average. How much rice and salt does he sell per month? . A family requires 1.25 litres of milk daily. If the price of a litre of milk is Tk. 52.00 per litre, how much does the family spend for milk in 30 days? The length and breadth of a rectangular garden is 60 metres and 40 metres respectively. There is a2 metres wide walkway around the inside of the garden. Determine the area of the walkway. The length of a house is three times of its breadth. The cost to cover the floor with a carpet is Tk. 1102.50 at the rate of Tk.7.50 per sq. metre. Determine the length and breadth of the house. The base and hight of a parallelogram are 40 m and 30 m respectively. (a) Define parallelogram along with drawing. (b) Determine the area of the parallelogram. (c ) If the area of the parallelogram is equal to the area of a square, find the perimeter of the square. D 2x c In the figure, the perimeter of the parallelogram ABCD is 30 metres. x 4 Metre A F B [ >2x < ] (a) Find out the length of the smallest side of the parallelogram. (b) Determine the area of the traingle ADF.', 499, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-3-008', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch3', 'en', 54, 55, 59, 60, 8, 'x 4 Metre A F B [ >2x < ] (a) Find out the length of the smallest side of the parallelogram. (b) Determine the area of the traingle ADF. (c) How many square centimeter is the area of the (0 BCDF. 2026 2026 Mathematics 55 Sample Questions Multiple Choice Questions: 1. 1sq.foot = how much sq.cm? (a) 729 square cm (b) 829 square cm (c) 929 square cm (d) 992 square cm If the side length of a square is 5 cm — (i) The length of its side is 50 millimeters (ii) Its perimeter is 20 cm (iii) Its area is 25 square cm Which of the following is correct? (a) i & ii (b) i & iii (c) ii & iti (d) i, ii & iti Answer to question no. 3 & 4 based on the following information: The length and breadth of a piece of land are 20 and 15 metres respectivly. 3. What is the perimeter of that piece of land in metre? (a) 35 (b) 70 (c) 140 (d) 300 2 metres wide walkway is made around inside the piece of land. How much metres is the area of the piece of land excluding the walkway? (a) 70 (b) 124 (c) 176 (d) 300 Creative Question: 5. The area of a rectangular garden is 2020 square meters. Around the inside of the garden, there is a 2-meter wide path along all four sides. Excluding the path, the length and width of the garden are 46 meters 50 cm and 36 meters respectively. Planting grass on the path costs 20 taka per square meter. (a) A container has a volume of 100 cubic centimeters. How many milliliters of water can it hold? (b) Calculate the total cost of planting grass on the path. (c) Determine the perimeter of the garden in feet. Short Answer Questions: (a) If the volume of a mug is 500 cubic centimeters, how many liters of water can it hold? (b) If a triangular plot of land has a base of 12 meters and a height of 8 meters, what is its area? (c) If your study table is 70 cm long and 60 cm wide, what is its area in square meters? (d) If your friend''s height is 152.4 cm, what is their height in feet