  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-003', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 118, 121, 123, 126, 3, 'of R.H.S. This is called the law of cross-multiplication. 118 or, or, or, or, or, Simple Equations 22 «2 3 Example 3. Solve : — —-—=—— 3 6 4 . 2Z «2 3 Solution : — — — =—-— 3 6 4 4z-z 3 ; ; ; 6 = “4 [In left hand side, L.C.M. of denominators 3 and 6 is 6] 3z_ 3 6 4 z_ 3 2 4 4x z=2.x(—3) [by cross-multiplication] 2x 2z=2x(-3) 2z=-3 [by cancelling the common factor tor 2 from both sides] or, or, or, 3 —_—= “5 [dividing both sides by 2] “. Solution: Z=——. (4) Law of symmetry Equation : 2x +1=5x-8 or, 5x —8=2x +1 All terms of L.H.S. can be transposed to R.H.S. and all terms of R.H.S. can be transposed to L.H.S. simultaneously without changing the sign of any term of any side. This is called the law of symmetry. 2026 2026 Mathematics 119 Applying the above mentioned axioms and laws, an equation can be transformed into an easy form and finally it takes the form x =a ; that is, the value of the variable x, is a determined. Example 4. Solve : 2(5+ x) =16. Solution : 2(5 + x) =16 or, 2x54+2xx=16 [by distributive law] or, 10+2x=16 or, 2x =16—10 [by transposing ] or, 2x=6 2x 6 _ . or, ——= = [dividing both sides by 2] 2 2 or, x=3. Solution x = 3 3x+7 5x-4 1 : =x Example 5. Solve : +3 7 2 Solution : 3x47 + Sx a4 _ x+3 u 7 2 3x47 + 5x=4 x 7 [by transposing ] > 4 7 > y posing 7(3x +7)—-4(5x-—4)-28x 7 [In left hand side, L.C.M. of the or, = denominators 4 and 7 is 28] 28 2 + _16— or, 21x + 49+ 20x 28x _7 [by distributive law] 28 2 13x+33 7 or, =— 28 2 or, 28x———=28x ; [ multiplying both sides by 28] 120 or, 13x+33=98 or, 13x =98-33 or, 13x=65 13x 65 or, —=— 13.13 or, x=5 Solution : x =5 Simple Equations [dividing both sides by 13] Activity : Solve : 1. 2x-1=0 2. 5 +153 3. 4(y—-3) =8 Exercise 7-1 Solve : 1. 4x+1=2x+7 2. S5x-3=2x+3 3. 3y+1l=7y-1 4. Ty-S=y-1 5. 17-2z=3z+2 6. 13z-5=3-22 7, 2ah 8. ~41=3 4 3 2 9. ~45=~47 10, 2-2 =2_= 3 2 2 3 5 6 un, ¥2-5¥ 4 12, 2-1 Ls 5 7 7 5 3 13. ox 4x 2 14. y-2 2y-l =y 1 7 5 5 7 4 3 3 15. 3yt+l_ 3y-7 16. x+1 x-2 x-3 5 3 2 3 5 17. 2(x+3)=10 18. 5(x-2) =3(x-4) 19. 7(33-2y)+5(y-l) =34 20. (z-1)(z+2) = (z+ 4)(z-2) 2026 Mathematics 121 7:3, Formation of simple equation and solution A customer wants to buy 3 kg of lump molasses. The shopkeeper measured half of a big lump molasses of x kg. But it weighted less than 3 kg. After adding 1 kg more, it weighted 3 kg.', 496, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-004', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 121, 123, 126, 128, 4, 'lump molasses. The shopkeeper measured half of a big lump molasses of x kg. But it weighted less than 3 kg. After adding 1 kg more, it weighted 3 kg. We want to find what was the weight of the whole lump(ie. big lump) of molasses, that is, what is the value of x? For this purpose we need to form an equation. Here, the equation will be 5 +1=3. If the equation is solved, value of x will be obtained ; that is, the weight of the whole lump molasses. Activity : Form the equations by the given information (one is done for you): Given information Equation 1. If 25 is subtracted from five times of a number xX, the difference will be 190. 2. Present age of a son is y years. His father’s age is ] y+4y =45 four times of his age and sum of their present ages is 45 years 3. Length of a rectangular pond is X metre, breadth is 3 metres less than the length and perimeter of the pond is 26 metres. Example 7. In an examination Ahona got total 176 marks in English and Mathematics and she got 10 marks more in Mathematics than in English. What marks did she obtain in each of the subjects ? Solution : Suppose, Ahona has got x marks in English. Therefore, she has got (x +10) marks in Mathematics By the question, x+x+10=176 or, 2x+10=176 or, 2x =176-10 [by transposing] or, 2x = 166 or, = = “ [dividing both sides by 2] or, x = 83 Class-7, Mathematics, Forma-16 122 Simple Equations ~ x+10=83+10=93 *, Ahona has got 83 marks in English and 93 marks in Mathematics. Example 8. Jamil bought some pens from a shop. From those pens, he gave + of the pens to his sister and + of those to his brother. 5 more pens were left with him. How many pens did he buy ? Solution : Let, Jamil bought x pens. 1 x 1 x “. He gave = of x or 5 pens to his sister and > of x or 3 pens to his 2 3 brother. By the condition of the problem, x— (= + *) =5 xX Xx or, x-—-—= 2 3 or, as =5 [InL.H.S., L.C.M. of denominators 2 and 3 is 6] or, —_ 5 6 or, x=5x6 _— [by cross-multiplication] or, x = 30 *, Jamil bought 30 pens. Example 9. A bus started from Gabtoli with the speed of 25 km per hour reached Aricha. Again, started from Aricha with the speed of 30 km per hour returned to Gabtoli. It took 55 hours in total for its plying between the two places. What is the distance between Gabtoli and Aricha ? 2026 2026 Mathematics 123 Solution : Suppose, distance between Gabtoli and Aricha is d km. ..', 478, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-005', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 123, 125, 128, 130, 5, 'plying between the two places. What is the distance between Gabtoli and Aricha ? 2026 2026 Mathematics 123 Solution : Suppose, distance between Gabtoli and Aricha is d km. .. The time to ply from Gabtoli to Aricha is < hours. . . . .. da Again, the time to ply from Aricha to Gabtoli is 39 0 .. The total time taken by the bus for its plying between the two places = & + <| hours. 25 30 According to the question, a + a =5 a 25 30 2 6d+5d_ 11 * 150 2 Hd = 150 u = x or, or, d=75 .. The distance between Gabtoli and Aricha is 75 km. Example 10 . The difference between two positive integers is 40 and their ratio is 1:3. (a) Form two equations considering the numbers x, y. (b) Determine the two numbers. (c) Assuming the two numbers as unit metre of length and breadth of rectangle, find perimeter and area of that rectangle. Solution : (a) Let, the two number be x and y, x>y As per question, x- y=40 .......... (i) And y:x = 1:3 y _1 i car .. Equations are, x-y = 40 or, x= 3y and x = 3y (b) Getting from ''a'' 124 From (7) and (if) we get, 3y—y=40 or, 2y =40 _ 40 or, Y= 5 = 20 Putting y= 20 in (ii) we get, x=3x20= 60 x= 60 .. The two numbers are 60 and 20 (c) Got from ''b'' The two number are 60 and 20. Let, the length of the rectangle be 60 metres The breadth » ” 20 ” .. Perimeter of rectangle = 2 (length + breadth ) = 2 (60 + 20) = 160 metres The area of the rectangle = length x breadth 60 m x 20m 1200 sq.m Simple Equations 2026 2026 Mathematics 125 Exercise 7-2 Form equations from the following problems and solve: 1. What is the number, if 5 is added to its twice, the sum will be 25 ? 2. What is the number, if 27 is subtracted from it, the difference will be — 21? 3. Of what number one-third will be equal to 4 ? 4. What is the number, if 5 is subtracted from it, 5 times the difference will be equal to 20 ? 5. What is the number if one-third of it is subtracted from its half, the difference will be 6 ? 6. Sum of three consecutive natural numbers is 63 ; find the numbers. 7. Sum of two numbers is 55 and 5 times the larger number is equal to 6 times of the smaller number. Determine the two numbers. 8. Deena, Meena and Rina together have 180 taka. Deena has 6 taka less and Rina has 12 taka more than that of Meena. How much money does each of them have? 9. Total price of an exercise book and a pen is 75 taka.', 494, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-006', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 125, 126, 130, 131, 6, 'Rina has 12 taka more than that of Meena. How much money does each of them have? 9. Total price of an exercise book and a pen is 75 taka. If the price of the exercise book would be less by 5 taka and the price of the pen be more by 2 taka, price of the exercise book would be twice the price of the pen. What are the prices of the exercise book and the pen ? 10. A fruit seller has some fruits of which 4 portion is apple, t portion is orange and 40 mangoes. How many fruits are there in total ? 11. The present age of a father is 6 timesof the present age of his son. After 5 years, sum of their ages will be 45 years. What are the present ages of father and son ? 12. Ratio of the ages of Sara and Saba is 2 : 3. If the sum of their ages is 30 years, what are their individual age ? 13. In a cricket match the total number of runs scored by Imon and Sumon was 58. Number of Imon’s run was 5 runs less than twice the number of Sumon’s run. What was the number of runs scored by Imon in that game ? 14. A train moving with the speed of 30 km per hour travelled from Kamalapur station to Narayangonj station. If the speed of the train was 25 km per hour, it would take 10 minutes more for the journey. What is the distance between the two stations ? 126 Simple Equations 15. The length of a rectangular land is thrice its breadth and the perimeter of the land is 40 metres. Determine the length and breadth of the land. Graphs 7-4 Concepts of co-ordinates Famous mathematician Rene Descartes (1596-1650) of France gave first the idea of co- ordinates. He explained about the position of a point with respect to two mutually perpendicular lines. y To know the position of a student in seating arrangement in a class-room, his position along a 7 G2) horizontal line as well as along a vertical line is to x : ats be known. Suppose we want to know the position of a student Liza (L) in her class-room. Liza’s position may be ¥ considered as a point (.). We observe in the picture that Liza’s position is at the point L which is at a distance of 3 units along the horizontal line OX from a fixed point O and from there it is at a distance of 2 units up along a vertical line parallel to OY . This position of Liza is expressed by (3, 2), 7-5 Plotting of points On a graph paper there are small equal squares made by horizontal and vertical parallel lines. To show the position of a point or to plot a point in the graph paper * is called plotting of points.', 493, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-007', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 126, 128, 131, 133, 7, 'squares made by horizontal and vertical parallel lines. To show the position of a point or to plot a point in the graph paper * is called plotting of points. For plotting of points, two mutually perpendicular straight lines are taken. _ Figure 3: on a graph paper, x-axis and y-axis In the figure, two mutual perpendicular lines xOX'' and YOY'' intersect each other at the point O. The point O is called the origin. Horizontal line YOX'' is the x-axis and vertical line YOY’ is the y -axis. 2026 2026 Mathematics 127 Mainly the length of the side of the smallest square in graph paper is considered as a unit. Generally, the co- Y ordinates of any point is written as (x,y), x is called [2fatadrait If qhuafiraht In Eh) TI) the x abscissa of the point and y is called the ordinate of the point. Obviously the co-ordinates of the origin O are (0, 0). x’ @) i From the origin, right side of x -axis is the positive side and its left side is negative side. Again, from the origin, |_lmlCi- Web : soe we : . 3# qhadrant A quadrant upper side of y -axis is positive side and the lower side of it is negative side. As a result, the graph is divided into four parts (quadrants). In anticlockwise direction, these four parts are known as first, second, third and << fourth quadrants respectively. In first quadrant, both x and y co-ordinates of any point are positive. In second quadrant, x of any point is negative and y is positive. In third quadrant both x and y of any point are negative. In fourth quadrant, x co-ordinate of any point is positive and y is negative. In the previous section, to find the position (3, 2) of Liza, first move 3 units of distance from origin towards the right side (positive side) along x axis. Then from that point, move 2 units of distance towards the upper side vertically. Then the co-ordinates of L (position of Liza) will be (3, 2). Similarly in the graphs, co-ordinates of the point P are (—4, 2). Example 1. Plot the following first four points on a graph paper following the given direction. (3,2) > (6,2) > (6,4) > 3,4). What will be the geometric shape of the figure ? Solution : Let the four points be A, B, C, D; respectively that is, AQ, 2), B(6, 2), C(6, 4) and D@, 4). In the graph paper we take the length of the side of the smallest square as unit. To plot the point 4, we take 3 units of length equal to sum of the lengths of three consecutive sides of the squares from the origin O along the right side of x-axis. Then from there we move vertically upwards by 2 units of length equal to the sum of two consecutive sides of the squares. 128 Simple Equations Thus we get a point which is A.', 499, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-7-008', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch7', 'en', 128, 129, 133, 134, 8, 'move vertically upwards by 2 units of length equal to the sum of two consecutive sides of the squares. 128 Simple Equations Thus we get a point which is A. Similarly we plot the given points. Then we join the points successively in the direction A > B > C > D— A. Thus we get the figure ABCD. We see that the figure ABCD is a rectangle. Activity : From the graph, find the co-ordinates of the points O, R, S,T. 7.6 Solution of equations by graphs Solution of equations can easily be found with the help of graphs. Suppose, the equation 2x —5 =0 is to be solved through graphs. If different values of x are put on left side 2x —5,we will get different values of the expression 2x — 5. In graphs, taking the values of x as abscissas and the corresponding values of 2x —S as ordinates weshall get different points. Joining the points, a straight line can be drawn. The abscissa of the point where the line intersects x -axis, will be the solution; because the expression 2x —5 becomes 0 (zero) for that value of x. Here the solution of the equation is x = : . Example 2. Solve 3x — 6 = 0 and show the solution in graphs. Solution : 3x —-6=0 or, 3x =6 [by transposition] or, 26 [dividing both sides by 3] 3. 3 or, X=2 Solution : x = 2 Drawing of graphs : Given equation is 3x -6 =0. Taking some different values of x, we find the corresponding values of 3x—6 and make the adjoining table: Three points (2, 0), (5,9) and (6,12) are taken to draw the graph. 2026 Mathematics 129 x 3x—6 | (x,3x—6) 2 0 (2, 0) 5 9 (5, 9) 6 12 (6, 12) Let, two mutually perpendicular lines XOX'' and YOY'' be x-axis and y -axis respectively and O be the origin. On graph paper, taking the length of one side of the smallest square on both axes as a unit, we plot the points (2,0), (5, 9), (6, 12). Then we join the points successively ; we get a straight line in the graph. The straight line intersects the x-axis at the point (2,0) whose abscissa is 2. Therefore, the solution of the given equation is x =2. Example 3. Solve by graphs : 3x -4=—-x+4. Solution : Given equation is 3x —-4=—-x+4. x) 3x—4 |(w3x-4) Taking some different values of x , we find the 2 corresponding values of 3x—4 and make the 0 4 (0, —4) adjoining table 1. 2 2 (2, 2) .. Take three points (0, — 4),(2, 2), (4, 8) on the 4 8 (4, 8) graph of 3x—4. Table-1 Again, taking some different values of x, we find the corresponding values of —x+4 and make the table -2 beside : X | -x+4/(x,-x+4) .', 481, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
