  ('class7-math-en-chunk-8-001', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch8', 'en', 133, 134, 138, 139, 1, '2026 Chapter Eight Parallel Straight Lines Some of the things we see or use in our everyday life are rectangular or circular shaped. The rooms we live in, doors and windows, chair, table, books etc. are of rectangular shape. If we consider the opposite edges of these objects as straight lines, they are parallel. At the end of this chapter, the students will be able to > Explain the properties of the angles made by parallel straight lines and a transversal > State the conditions of parallelism of two straight lines. > Prove the conditions of parallelism of two straight lines. 8-1 Geometrical argument method Proposition : The subjects discussed in Geometry is generally called a proposition. Construction: The proposition in which one has to draw geometrical figure and prove its validity by arguments is called Construction. Parts of a construction: a) Data: The given facts in the construction are data . b) Steps of construction: The drawings which are made to solve the problem. c) Proof: Justification of the construction by arguments. Theorem: The proposition which is established for some geometrical statement by arguments is called a theorem. The theorem consists of the following parts: a) General enunciation: This is a preliminary statement describing in general terms the purpose of the proposition. b) Particular enunciation: This repeats in special terms the statement already made, and refers it to a diagram, which enables the reader to follow the reasoning more easily. c) Construction: The additional drawing made to prove the truth of a problem. d) Proof: The proof shows that the object proposed in a problem has been all accomplished, or that the property stated in a theorem is true. Corollary: This is a statement of the truth of which follows readily from an established proposition as an inference or deduction, which usually requires no further proof. 134 Parallel Straight Lines For discussion of modern deductive geometry some basic propositions, definitions and symbols are required. The symbols used in Geometry Symbol Meaning Symbol Meaning + addition Z angle = equal to L is perpendicular to > is greater than A triangle < is less than @ circle = is congruent to . since ll is parallel to a therefore 8-2 Transversals A transversal is a straight line that intersects two or more straight lines at different points. In the figure, AB and CD are any two straight lines and the straight line LM intersects them at P and Q respectively. The line LM is a transversal of the lines AB and CD. The transversal has made eight angles with the lines AB and CD which are denoted by 41,22, 23,24, 25,26,27, 28. The angles may be classified as internal and external or corresponding and alternate. 2026', 456, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-8-002', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch8', 'en', 135, 136, 140, 141, 2, 'eight angles with the lines AB and CD which are denoted by 41,22, 23,24, 25,26,27, 28. The angles may be classified as internal and external or corresponding and alternate. 2026 2026 Mathematics 135 Internal angles 23,24,25, 26 External angles 21,22,27, 28 Pairs of corresponding angles 1 and 25,72 and 26 3 and 47,24 and 28 Pairs of internal alternate angles 3 and 76,74 and Z5 Pairs of external alternate angles 1 and 28,72 and Z7 Pairs of internal angles on one side of the transversal 3and 25,24 and 46 Properties of corresponding angles: (a) have different angular points of the angles (b) are on the same side of the transversal Properties of alternate interior angles: (a) have different angular points of the angles (b) are on opposite sides of the transversal an (c) lie between the two lines. Activity 1. (a) Name the pairs of angles in each figure. (b) Identify the corresponding angles of 23 and 26. (c) Identify the vertical opposite angle of 74 and the angle supplementary to Ez 8.3 Pair of parallel straight lines We have already known that if two straight lines in a plane do not intersect each other, they are parallel. If we take segments of these two parallel straight lines, they are also parallel. The perpendicular distance of any point on any of these lines to the other is always the same. Conversely, if the perpendicular distances from any two points of a straight line to the other are equal, the straight lines are parallel. This perpendicular distance is known as the distance between the two parallel lines. > l tb, ti, Note that, through a point not on a line only a single parallel line can be drawn. 136 Parallel Straight Lines 8.4 Angles made by bisect of parallel lines with a transversal In the figure above, the straight line EF intersects the lines 4B and CD at P and Q respectively. The line EF is a transversal of the lines AB and CD. The transversal has made a total of eight angles 21, 72, 23, 24, 25, 26, Z7, Z8 with the lines AB and CD. Among the angles (a) Zland 25,22 and6, Z3and 27,24 and Z8 are mutually corresponding angles. (b) Z3 and 26, 74 and Z5 are mutually alternate angles. (c) 23,24, 25, Z6 are internal angles. These alternate angles related to each other; so are the corresponding angles. Do the following group activity to find out the relations. Activity : 1. Ona ruled sheet of paper draw two parallel lines and a transversal as like as the figure below. Identify two pairs of corresponding angles. Verify whether each pair of corresponding angles are equal. Are they really equal? 2. Identify two pairs of alternate angles. Verify whether each pair of corresponding angles are equal. Are they really equal? 3. Measure the pair of interior angles on the same side of the transversal. Find the sum of the two angles.', 492, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-8-003', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch8', 'en', 136, 138, 141, 143, 3, 'of corresponding angles are equal. Are they really equal? 3. Measure the pair of interior angles on the same side of the transversal. Find the sum of the two angles. Compare the sum with the sum done by your peers. Is your sum around 180°? t A! 2026 Mathematics 137 As a result of the group activity we reach the following conclusion: When a transversal cuts two parallel straight lines, the corresponding angles are equal. When a transversal cuts two parallel straight lines, the alternate angles are equal. When a transversal cuts two parallel straight lines, that pair of interior angles on the same side of the transversal are supplementary, These three properties of parallel lines cannot be proved separately. These are all variations of Euclid''s 5th theorem. Considering either of these as definitions of parallel straight lines, the remaining two natures can be explained. That is, if any one of these three properties can be explained as true by the other two natures, then we can assume the first definition to be correct. A Nature of Parallel Lines: Another property of parallel lines is explained below, assuming that every pair of corresponding angles formed by an intersection of two parallel lines is equal. The ratio of the angles formed by an intersection of two parallel straight lines: Theorem 1 If a straight line intersects two parallel straight lines, the alternate angles are equal. Particular Enunciation: Let AB || CD and the transversal PQ intersect them at E and F respectively. It is required to prove that ZAEF = alternate ZEFD | Proof: Steps Justification (1) ZPEB = Corresponding ZEFD [According to the definition of parallel (2) ZPEB = Vertically opposite lines, corresponding angles are equal.] ZAEF [ Vertically opposite angles are equal] Therefore, ZAEF =ZEFD (yr oved) [From (1) and (2)] Activity : 1. Prove that when a transversal cuts two parallel straight lines, the sum of interior angles on the same side of the transversal is two right angles. Class-7, Mathematics, Forma-18 138 Parallel Straight Lines In the figure AB || CD and the transversal PQ P intersects them at E and F'' respectively. A Therefore, (a) ZAEF =Corresponding ZEFD (b) ZPEB = alternate <EF''D (c) ZBEF + ZEFD = 2 right angles. Activity : 1. Take two points on a straight line. Draw two 60° angles at these points in the same direction. Verify whether the drawn sides of the angles are parallel. 2. > or’ Find the value of angles made by the transversales. As aresult of considering of the activity we reach at the following conclusion: When a transversal cuts two lines, such that the pairs of corresponding angles are equal, the lines have to be parallel. When a transversal cuts two lines, such that the pairs of alternate interior angles are equal, the lines have to be parallel.', 474, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-8-004', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch8', 'en', 138, 141, 143, 146, 4, 'are equal, the lines have to be parallel. When a transversal cuts two lines, such that the pairs of alternate interior angles are equal, the lines have to be parallel. When a transversal cuts two lines, such that the pairs of interior angles on the same side of the transversal are supplementary, the lines have to be parallel. In the figure the transversal PQ intersects the P straight lines at F and F respectively and (a) ZAEF = alternate ZEFD or, (b) ZPEB = Corresponding ZEFD or, (c) ZBEF + ZEFD= 2 right angles. Therefore, the straight lines AB and CD are parallel. 2026 2026 Mathematics 139 Exercise 8 1. In the figure ZPOR =55°, ZLRN =90° P N M and PQ || MR. Which one of the following is the value of Z MRN ? L R (a) 35° (b) 45° (c) 55° (d) 90° 2. In the figure, if POIISR, PO= PR P S and ZPRO=50; what is the value of ZLRS? Q R L (a) 80° (b) 75° (c) 55° (d) 50° 3. Inthe figure, AB II CD Il EF A E (3) Which one of the following is the value of 2x? (a) 28° (b) 32° (c) 45° (d) 58° (ii) | Which one of the following is the value of zz? (a) 58° (b) 103° (c) 122° (d) 148° (iii) Which one of the following is the value of y-z? (a) 58° (b) 77° (c) 103° (d) 122° 4, What is meant by = (a) Equal (b) congruent (c) Parallel (d) Perpendicular 140 Answer to the questions no. 5 & 6 Parallel Straight Lines based on the following figure. wos (SV LY) 1\ 5. x=? (a) 75° (b) 55° (c) 50° (d) 45° 6. xty=? (a) 160° (b) 125° (c) 100° (d) 85° 7. Inthe figure, AB IICD, ZBPE = 60° E and PQ = PR. A Pp 8 c 7 D (a) Show that 5 CAPE = 60° (b) Determine the value of ZCOF’ (c) Prove that, POR is an equilateral triangle. Sample Questions Multiple Choice Questions: 1. If ZB+2ZC=90, then in AABC, 24=? (a) 90° (b) 110° (c) 120° (d) 160° 2. In the adjacent figure, AB || CD, and line segment PQ intersects AB and CD at points Rand S respectively. According to the figure — i. ZPRB corresponds to ZRSD ii. ZARS is alternate to ZRSD iii. ZCSQ + ZCSR = 180° Which of the following is correct? (a)i & ii (b) i & iti (c) ii & tii (d) i, ti & iti 2026 2026 Mathematics Answer to the questions no. 3 & 4 based on the following figure. P A = 50° VE B AB\|CD C Fs D Q 3. What is the measurement of ZPEA? (a) 40° (b) 50° (c) 90° (d) 130° 4. What is the measurement of ZEFD ? (a) 30° (b) 40° (c) 50° (d) 90° Creative Question: 5. Inthe figure, PQ || RS and AB is their transversal.', 494, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-8-005', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch8', 'en', 141, 141, 146, 146, 5, '130° 4. What is the measurement of ZEFD ? (a) 30° (b) 40° (c) 50° (d) 90° Creative Question: 5. Inthe figure, PQ || RS and AB is their transversal. (a) If the complement of Zx is 25°, find the value of Zx. (b) Prove that ZPEF = ZEFS. (c) Show that ZPEF + ZEFR = 180°. Short Answer Questions: 141 6. (a) BC and DE are two parallel straight lines intersected by line segment PQ at points L and M respectively. Draw a figure and identify a pair of alternate interior angles formed at points L and M. (b) If line PQ intersects line segment AB at point B, draw a figure and identify the pair of adjacent angles formed at the point of intersection. (c) If AC and BD are two parallel straight lines intersected by line segment LM at points P and Q respectively, draw a figure and calculate the sum of the interior angles on the right side of the transversal LM.', 166, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-9-001', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch9', 'en', 142, 143, 147, 148, 1, 'Chapter Nine Triangles We have already known that the figure bounded by three line segments is a triangle and the line segments are known as the sides of the triangle. The point common to any two sides is known as vertex. The angle formed at the vertex is an angle of the triangle. Thus, the triangle has three sides and three angles. Depending on the lengths of the sides,the triangles are of three types: equilateral, isosceles and scalene. Again, on the basis of the angles, the triangles are also of three types: acute angled, obtuse angled and right angled. The sum of the lengths of the sides is called perimeter of the triangle. In this chapter the basic theorems and constructions related to triangle are discussed. At the end of this chapter, the students will be able to - > Discribe the interior and exterior angles of a triangle; Prove the fundamental theorems related to triangles; Construct triangles from given conditions; Solve real life problems using the relations of the sides and the angles; VV V WV Measure the area by measuring the base and height of the region of the triangles. 9-1 Medians of a triangle In the figure beside ABC is a triangle with *&k A vertices A,B,C and angles 7A, ZB, ZC. AB, f BC CA are the three sides of the triangle. Consider any one of its sides, say, BC and B Cc locate the mid-point D of BC . Join AD. D The line segment AD, joining the mid-point of BC to its opposite vertex 4A is a median of x the triangle. A median connects a vertex of a triangle to the midpoint of the opposite side. 2026 2026 Mathematics 143 9.2 Altitudes of a triangle In the adjointed figure, ABC is a triangle. The A height is the distance from vertex A to the base BC. The height is given by the line segment that starts from A, goes straight down to BC, and is perpendicular to BC. This line segment AM is an altitude of the triangle. Thus an altitude can be B Cc determined through each vertex of the triangle, this way. 9.3 Exterior and interior angles of a triangle interior opposite angles 4 If any side of a triangle is extended, the angle formed is an exterior angle. The two angles other than the adjacent interior angle are known as the interior opposite angles. B exterior angle x C ny interior adjacent angle In the adjoint figure, the side BC of AABC is produced to D. Observe the angle ZACD formed at the point C. This angle lies in the exterior of ZABC. We call it an exterior angle of the ZABC formed at vertex C. Clearly ZACB is an adjacent angle to ZACD. The remaining two angles of the triangle namely ZABC and ZBAC are called the two interior opposite angles or the two remote interior angles of ZACD. Activity : 1. How many medians does a triangle have?', 500, '06-Mathematics.pdf', 'tesseract (pdftoppm -gray -r 200, -l eng)', 'OCR text; equations/stacked fractions/geometry symbols do not survive OCR reliably and are not quoted as exact. No confidence score produced by tesseract CLI in this run.'),
  ('class7-math-en-chunk-9-002', 'class7-math-en', 'class7-math-en-ch9', 'en', 143, 145, 148, 150, 2, 'triangle namely ZABC and ZBAC are called the two interior opposite angles or the two remote interior angles of ZACD. Activity : 1. How many medians does a triangle have? How many altitudes? 2. Do a median and an altitude lie entirely in the interior of the triangle? 3. Draw a triangle whose altitude and median are on the same line segment. 9.4 Sum of three angles of a triangle There is a remarkable property connecting the three angles of a triangle. We are going to see this through the three following activities. Activity : 1. Draw a triangle. Cut on the three angles. Rearrange them as shown in Fig (ii). The three angles now constitute one angle. This angle is a straight angle and measures 180°. Thus, the sum of the measures of the three angles of a triangle is 180°. 0) Gai) 144 Triangles 2. Draw a triangle and make two copies if it. Arrange the three copies as shown in the figure. What do you observe about three angles seen together? Da they make a straight angle? La \ 3. Draw any three triangles in your notebook according to your choice. Use your protractor and measure each of the angles of these triangles. Tabulate your results one is done for you. Triangle Measures of Angles Sum of the three angles In AABC ZA = 60° ZB = 65° | ZA+ZB+ ZC = 1809 y ZC =55° Do you find that the sum of the three angles always gives around 180° Theorem 1 The sum of the three angles of a triangle is equal to two right angles. A E > B Cc D Particular Enunciation: Let ABC be a triangle. It is required to prove that ZBAC + ZABC + ZACB = 2 right angles. Construction: Extend BC to D and draw CE parallel to BA. 2026 Mathematics 145 Proof: Steps Justification (1) ZBAC =ZACE [BA and CE are parallel and AC is a transversal] [*. the alternate angles are equal] (2) ZABC =ZECD [BA and CE are parallel and BD is a transversal] [‘. the corresponding angles are equal] (3) ZBAC + ZABC = ZACE + ZECD = Z